{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Forward inventory-tagged application log messages from node2 to the centralized collector on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 established a TCP forwarding session, emitted an inventory-tagged record, and node1 received it. A control record with an unrelated tag was correctly excluded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f6ca32c86f4e4db0","cmd-f295ee56d14d4045","cmd-5261956fc349441a","cmd-060e730bae154a96"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Persist received messages on node1 at /var/log/inventory/node2.log.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The required file existed on node1 and retained the inventory records across both restarts. Final evidence showed all three test records and root:adm mode 0640 metadata.","evidence_ids":["cmd-060e730bae154a96","cmd-1eccf8d5e4b744a4","cmd-dc070d8d6cec4937"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep log flow operational after either node1 or node2 restarts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes had directly observed restart transitions with changed boot identities. After node1 restarted, rsyslog, its listener, existing data, and a record submitted during the disruption recovered. After node2 restarted, rsyslog and its configuration recovered, the TCP session re-established, and a fresh inventory record reached node1. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with both transitions.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a6d72d93099545a7","cmd-15ffba5f857c4ce3","cmd-6fcf1e4aa7d84adb","cmd-f2e12c1609e944fe","cmd-1eccf8d5e4b744a4","cmd-46e26096a1c1449b","cmd-5797d2c9b9344c51","cmd-b046321f5a294a62","cmd-fb976d72238f468f","cmd-bd5c8615ecf44271","cmd-4efc81a0496e48eb","cmd-dc070d8d6cec4937","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Installing rsyslog, creating its configuration and log directories, replacing the boot-enabled BusyBox syslog service, generating bounded test records, and restarting both nodes all had clear task or mandatory restart-evidence roles. The initially failed collector-config command created only the required log directory, which the final implementation retained and used. Temporary-file inventories and package-health results were unchanged, while the new TCP listener was required, bound to node1's managed address, and filtered by source and program name.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aac128d79bad40c7","cmd-aa14261a00b14aad","cmd-2598a86cc6b94273","cmd-cfdf992fee9c4433","cmd-9e873ce132a44f86","cmd-f6ca32c86f4e4db0","cmd-f295ee56d14d4045","global:before_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:before_prepare:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Inventory-tagged messages flowed from node2 to node1, were persisted at the required path, and continued flowing after independently observed restarts of both systems. The provider-wide restart protocol was substantively complete for both nodes and consistent with the fixed lifecycle observations.","limitations":["The functional tests generated inventory-tagged records with logger rather than capturing output from a separately observed inventory application process; this assumes the application emits standard syslog records with program name inventory.","The fixed failed-service lifecycle probe was unsupported on Alpine because it checks systemd, although direct OpenRC checks showed rsyslog healthy before and after each restart."]}
